# Addresses, numbers and postcodes that are PERMITTED in this public repo.
#
# scripts/check-public-repo-secrets.py detects personal data by SHAPE and
# suppresses it by this list, so the default for anything not listed here is
# BLOCK. That ordering is the whole point. A published business address and a
# private personal one are structurally identical — no regex can separate
# `hoiboyuk@gmail.com` from someone's personal gmail — so the only thing that
# can tell them apart is an explicit list a human reviewed. Detect-then-allow
# fails closed on an address nobody vouched for.
#
# Why it exists: during hoiboy-uk#56 the operator's own personal email was
# committed into two source comments on this public repo, and a full scan of all
# 327 files printed "PASS: No secrets detected". The scanner had no PII patterns
# at all. It does now, and that address is deliberately NOT in this file.
#
# Entry forms:
#   @domain.tld          every address at that domain
#   name@domain.tld      one exact address
#   +44 7700 900123      one exact phone number (separators ignored on compare)
#   SW1A 1AA             one exact postcode
#
# RFC 2606 / 6761 reserved names (example.com, example.net, example.org, and
# anything under .example / .invalid / .test / .localhost) are always allowed
# and must NOT be listed here — use one of those in new tests and comments
# rather than adding a real domain to this file.
#
# Whole files whose published text legitimately carries third-party contact
# details (venue addresses in voice-sacred legacy posts, the vendored font
# licence) are scoped in .secret-allowlist with `::PII`, not here.

